  set:  # 8038
    title: "survey does not finish on nextjs: crypto-flow-linker masks js-module-linker's binary-asset file nodes (.png/.jpg/.ttf stamped javascript) with an O(k^2) tree-sitter child(i) walk; 20k real files take 7.5 s, single images 28-150 s+"  # 8038
- op: discuss  # c7ea
  at: '2026-10-03T01:17:21Z'  # c7ea
  by: agent  # c7ea
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c7ea
  clock: 4  # c7ea
  nonce: c7ea  # c7ea
  message: "PREMISE RE-CHECK 2026-10-02 (dev 273034f369; owner-requested staleness/premise audit). Record: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/perf_kakov_rukaz_10022026/ (probes, logs, py-spy profile nextjs4.pyspy.txt, benches).\n\nCONDITIONS: four nextjs surveys (v1 cold, v2/v4/v5 warm), each through `slot run survey-big`, some concurrently, partly during the Proxmox host's daily backup. Every one was stopped by me (explicit PID); none wrote a survey. Wall-clock figures are therefore indicative only. The findings below rest on CPU-bound phases, stack samples and per-file timings, not on totals.\n\nTITLE CONDITION STILL HOLDS on current dev: the survey does not finish. Each run reaches the linkers in ~13 min (warm) and then stays in ONE linker. The cause is LOCATED and has three parts:\n\n1. **crypto-flow-linker parses BINARY ASSETS as JavaScript.** Its file list is every symbol path whose `language` is javascript/typescript/rust (crypto_flow.py ~235). js-module-linker mints `kind=file` nodes for every imported asset and stamps them with the IMPORTER's language. A copy of nextjs's test/integration/image-optimizer/app (1.4 MB) yields 15 such nodes: `file javascript ['js-module-linker'] public/animated2.png`, .gif, .webp, .avif, .jpg, .svg, .ico, .bmp... This is the WI-babuk mechanism reaching non-code targets (note on WI-babuk).\n   In-run per-file timing (v5 probe wrapping _scan_file_for_crypto_patterns): the first 20,000 real source files took **7.5 s in total**. Then: animated2.png (550 KB) 27.5 s, mountains.jpg (294 KB) 5.8 s, opengraph-image.png (950 KB) 49.6 s, examples/with-sfcc/public/hero.jpg (672 KB, `lang=typescript`) 42.4 s. After that, one file ran for 11+ min, still unfinished when I stopped the run.\n   Standalone, masking all 837 binary assets in nextjs as javascript took 434 s for the first 832. The five largest (>950 KB) were still running past 10 min. A 475 KB .ttf alone took 148 s, so the cost is not a function of size. These figures are an UPPER bound on the set, since not every asset is imported.\n\n2. **The masking walk is O(k^2) in a node's child count.** linkers/_text_filters.py:298-299 `_collect_mask_ranges` iterates `for i in range(node.child_count): node.child(i)`. tree-sitter's ts_node_child(i) walks the siblings from the start. A py-spy --native profile over 5 min inside crypto-flow (6,283 samples) puts **98% of them in tree-sitter's sibling iteration** (ts_subtree_extra 4,123, ts_subtree_padding 921, ts_subtree_visible, ts_node__relevant_child_count...) below that line. Binary input parses into very wide ERROR nodes, which is what makes k large. Real source files are fast: 20,067 nextjs source files took 15 s standalone, and a 10M-dict live heap or gc.freeze() made no difference (GC ruled out).\n   crypto-flow is the ONLY caller that passes `language=` to read_masked_source/mask_doc_regions. Every other caller gets the masker's no-parser fast path, which is why only this linker is affected.\n\n3. **crypto-flow masks BEFORE its keyword bailout** (`_BAILOUT_JS = (\"crypto.subtle\",)`, crypto_flow.py:150). Running the bailout on raw text first would skip the parse for nearly every file.\n\nON THE VSCODE DATA (10-01 note): NOT re-measured. Two mechanisms found on flink/nestjs fit it. (a) The \"~8 fds open, rotating\" signature matches compute_symbol_mention_centrality_batch: 8 threads, with `re` holding the GIL for a whole findall, so 7 threads wait holding open fds (observed directly on flink; see WI-rukaz). (b) rchar 92.9 GB, ~500x the repository's size, matches WI-fojot (`_classify_symbols` re-reads each whole file once per symbol; 40x byte amplification on nestjs). rchar is cumulative, so it includes earlier phases.\n\nOTHER FACTS FROM THESE RUNS: detect_profile took ~9 min cold under the backup, against 73 s on 17 MB nestjs. It does one full tree walk per LANGUAGE_EXTENSIONS entry (89 in all). That is a lead only: it is I/O-dominated, and these runs could not separate it from the backup. NOT reached in any nextjs run: the centrality scan (WI-rukaz's located bottleneck), which nextjs's symbol count makes a likely second wall.\n\nSTALE IN THE DESCRIPTION: the title's \"26 minutes at 3.1 GB\" (10-01 data: 3 h 05 min at 4.8 GB). The listed suspects (`crates/` Rust tree, minified bundles) are cleared. The largest bundle, packages/next/src/compiled/webpack/bundle5.js (2.6 MB), masks in 0.65-2 s, and analyzers + linkers apart from crypto-flow finish in ~13 min warm.\n\nFIX DIRECTIONS (not implemented, not measured): (i) js-module-linker should stamp an asset target with no language, or the asset's own, never the importer's (WI-babuk's prescribed fix is insufficient here; see the note there). (ii) crypto-flow should select files by source extension and run the bailout on raw text before masking. (iii) _collect_mask_ranges should use `node.children` (one O(k) call) instead of `child(i)`; the TreeCursor route was OOM-killed in CI under WI-tohod. Analogue sweep (rule 3): the same index-based shape, `field_name_for_child(i)` inside a child loop, appears in go_def_use.py:197, java_def_use.py:165 and c_def_use.py:140/150. Those see only real source, and I have not measured whether they matter.\n\nRetitled in this update; status and priority unchanged."  # c7ea
